    "contentHtml": "<h1>Supplement Evidence by Benefit</h1>\n<p>Evidence summaries powering the Common Sense Dog AI assistant, grouped by what each supplement supports. Confidence ratings reflect the strength of canine-specific research. Related: [[nutrition_reference]].</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>⚠️ Educational, not veterinary advice.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<h2>🦴 Joint Support — Green-Lipped Mussel</h2>\n<p><em>Perna canaliculus</em>, a New Zealand shellfish; marine omega-3s + glycosaminoglycans that modulate the COX/LOX inflammatory pathways (inhibits PGE2, like a milder NSAID).</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Evidence: MEDIUM, mixed.</strong> Hielm-Björkman 2009 (n=45 RCT): beat placebo on pain/mobility over 8 wks — but <strong>less than carprofen</strong>. Corbee 2022 (n=32 crossover): improved over baseline but <strong>not vs placebo</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Form matters</strong> — positive research used concentrated stabilized lipid extracts (<strong>PCSO-524</strong>, e.g. Antinol), not plain dried powder.</li>\n<li><strong>Krill combo</strong> (EAB-277/Antinol Rapid) showed effectiveness in a blinded canine study; whether it beats GLM alone isn&#39;t established.</li>\n<li>Slow-acting (8–16 wks consistent use); generally well-tolerated; caution with shellfish sensitivity.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>🔥 Anti-Inflammatory — Curcumin / Turmeric</h2>\n<p><strong>Turmeric</strong> = the whole spice; <strong>curcumin/curcuminoids</strong> = the small active fraction. Research uses concentrated extracts, not kitchen turmeric.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Evidence: LOW-to-MEDIUM.</strong> Comblain 2017 (n=42, 3-mo RCT): significant reduction in pain-on-manipulation, but <strong>flat objective gait</strong> (force-plate) measures.</li>\n<li><strong>Mechanism: HIGH</strong> — downregulates TNF-α, NF-kB, IL-8, COX-2 (PTGS2) in cell/in-vivo studies.</li>\n<li><strong>Bioavailability is the core problem</strong> — plain curcumin is poorly absorbed/rapidly cleared. Needs enhancement: phosphatidylcholine-bound, or piperine (black pepper) + fat. &quot;Golden paste&quot; is sound in principle but unvalidated in dogs and hard to standardize (LOW).</li>\n<li>Dose loosely 50–250 mg curcumin 3×/day (Wynn 2007); product curcuminoid content varies widely. Safe; watch blood-thinner interactions + GI upset at high doses.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>🦠 Gut &amp; Digestive — Fecal Microbiota Transplant (FMT)</h2>\n<p>Transfer of processed donor stool (enema or oral capsule) to correct <strong>dysbiosis</strong> (low gut bacterial diversity, measured via &quot;dysbiosis index&quot;).</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Evidence: MEDIUM overall, stronger for acute disease.</strong> 2025 prospective (n=20 chronic enteropathy): clinical index 5→1 at 1 mo, 17/20 still improved at 3 mo, some stable to 1 yr; improved bile acids + propionate. <strong>But</strong> Toresson 2022 (n=13 IBD, blinded): FMT = placebo (both also on steroids + hypoallergenic diet → improvement likely from concurrent therapy).</li>\n<li><strong>Best evidence in acute/infectious GI</strong> — parvo puppies (faster recovery, less hospitalization), superior to metronidazole at correcting post-diarrhea dysbiosis. Weaker/mixed for chronic IBD.</li>\n<li>No protocol standard yet; safe with rigorous donor screening; vet-supervised. Durability long-term under-studied.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>🛡️ Immune &amp; Gut — Bovine Colostrum</h2>\n<p>First milk after calving; rich in immunoglobulins (IgG), growth factors (IGF-1, TGF-β), antimicrobial proteins, proline-rich polypeptides (PRPs).</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Evidence: MEDIUM (note: industry-funded).</strong> Satyaraj 2013 (n=24, 40-wk RCT, Purina-funded): 0.1% spray-dried colostrum → stronger/longer vaccine antibody response, ↑ fecal IgA, ↑ gut microbiota diversity.</li>\n<li><strong>Gut</strong> — ↑ fecal IgA + microbiota stability; improved stool quality in weaned puppies; growth factors support gut-lining repair. Oligosaccharides act as a <strong>prebiotic</strong> (feeds beneficial bacteria, complements probiotics).</li>\n<li><strong>Immune</strong> — Igs survive gastric transit (powder/capsule); PRPs modulate immunity bidirectionally.</li>\n<li>Senior benefit is theoretical (key trial mean age 2.5 yr) → LOW-to-MEDIUM for that claim. Limits: modest samples, industry funding, much mechanism from pigs/humans. Watch dairy-intolerant dogs.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>🧘 Calming / Anxiety — L-Theanine</h2>\n<p>Amino acid from green tea; promotes alpha brain-wave/calm-but-alert state via neurotransmitter influence. <strong>Non-sedating</strong> (key appeal — dog stays responsive, good for training through triggers).</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Evidence: LOW-to-MEDIUM.</strong> Pike 2015 (open-label, storm-sensitive): owners reported less drooling/pacing/panting/hiding. Michelazzi (n=20 noise-phobia, 63-day + behavioral therapy): significant behavior improvement — <strong>but no change in blood cortisol</strong> (mixed signal).</li>\n<li>Weak designs: mostly open-label, owner-rated (caregiver placebo effect), small n (~10–20), some manufacturer-funded.</li>\n<li><strong>Best for</strong> situational/noise anxiety (storms, fireworks), not severe/generalized — and <strong>as part of a plan</strong> with behavioral training; melatonin can complement for nighttime. Sustained daily use (weeks / lead into anxiety season) beats single last-minute dose. Match dose to size (some chews underdose big dogs). Wide safety margin.</li>\n</ul>\n"
